WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Cookbook for Benchmarking Products and Services

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.400
Welcome back to this session on benchmarking.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.833
What we’re going to look at, is how we do a benchmarking study.

4
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:16.866
It’s really pretty fundamentally simple on how we do quantitative benchmarking,

5
00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:23.899
or Sigma benchmarking, quality benchmarking. Step number one is we go out and we define

6
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:30.466
the processes that we want to benchmark.

7
00:00:30.466 --> 00:00:36.732
Step two, for each process we define what is critical.

8
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:43.699
What things must go right. And for each one of those critical elements,

9
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:50.733
we count the number of defects.

10
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:57.466
And get the total number of units, establish our defect rate,

11
00:00:57.466 --> 00:01:04.499
and then convert that information to the Sigma scale

12
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.500
of measure. And finally, step five is we plot Sigma's.

13
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:18.466
Now, that's really pretty simple. What I wind up with is a simple graph

14
00:01:18.466 --> 00:01:25.432
of DPMO,

15
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:30.266
defects per million opportunities. By Sigma.

16
00:01:30.266 --> 00:01:37.232
For example, One Sigma, Two, Three, Four, Five, Six.

17
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:43.599
And the line that converts our DPMO into Sigma.

18
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.933
Then for all of those defined processes

19
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:54.899
and critical to quality characteristics. I plot them.

20
00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:01.900
On my chart.

21
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:08.866
And, I look at where the centroid of that tends to be.

22
00:02:08.866 --> 00:02:12.199
In this case, right about in here.

23
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:18.300
And that tells me overall what my Sigma capability is. Now, here's what's interesting.

24
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:25.300
Suppose, I go out and I do that in my processes, and I find a little group over here.

25
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.866
That's a pretty big difference

26
00:02:29.866 --> 00:02:33.632
in defects per million opportunities between these two.

27
00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:39.166
Well, what I’d do is go over here and seek those few out. And ask myself the question,

28
00:02:39.166 --> 00:02:44.632
what are they doing fundamentally different in identifying

29
00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:49.566
their critical X’s and controlling those critical X’s,

30
00:02:49.566 --> 00:02:53.466
in order to make their quality so much better?

31
00:02:53.466 --> 00:03:00.432
Then what I would do is transfer that understanding back into this group.

32
00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:05.566
Through teaching, etc.

33
00:03:05.566 --> 00:03:09.766
That's how we go about using benchmarking to create improvements.

34
00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:14.466
Understand where we're at and where we need to go.

35
00:03:14.466 --> 00:03:19.466
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
